-- The moves a player may make, as declarations rather than as predicates.
--
-- Sections are `docs/rules/rulemanual.txt`: §7-3-4 the free timing of the Main
-- Phase, §7-2-11 the charge, §7-3-5 the end of the Main Phase, §0-1-3-4
-- conceding.
--
-- An action is `WHEN / FOR / COST / DO / REFUSE` (§3b of
-- `docs/arena-rules-language.md`, "how an action is declared" in
-- `docs/arena-ruleset-spec.md`). `vm/actions.ts` reads all five: `WHEN` and
-- `prompts:` say which question it is offered at, `FOR` which cards it is
-- offered for, `REFUSE` the requirement a player is owed for each card it is
-- *not* offered for, and `COST` and `DO` what taking it costs and does. The
-- menu and the refusals therefore come from one paragraph, which is the whole
-- point of the file: nothing below has a hand-written `whyNot` twin.
--
-- **Seven so far.** The four that need no payment and no battle came first
-- (#145), which is what makes them the ones that proved the machinery on a real
-- turn; the prices followed (#148), and the play family below is the first to
-- carry one. Activations are #147 and the battle is Stage 6, and each arrives
-- here as a paragraph rather than as a branch somewhere else.
--
-- `listed: false` is the rule the legacy engine has always had and never
-- wrote down: a move a client shows as a button of its own is accepted
-- without ever being enumerated. It says nothing about legality — an unlisted
-- action is checked exactly as a listed one is — only that it is on neither
-- the menu nor the list of refusals, because a refusal explains a move a
-- player can see. `pass` and `concede` are the two.

DEFINE ACTION charge                                                      -- §7-2-11
  WHEN [charge]
  prompts: [charge]
  FOR 1 IN you.hand
  BIND "card"
  -- 7-2-11 is a *may*, and the half of it that takes no card is an answer of
  -- its own rather than the absence of one: a candidate, last on the menu, so
  -- the board keeps the ghost button beside the cards it could have charged
  -- (`docs/arena-hud-spec.md` §2.3). The `DO` below is written about the
  -- candidate, so declining runs it over no cards and moves nothing.
  decline: "Skip charge"
  DO {
    moveTo(target: $card, to: energy, reveal: true)
  }
  -- 3-1-2: a Leader Card does not leave the Leader Area, so it is not a card
  -- this move can be made about. Nothing puts one in a hand — 6-2-1-2 places it
  -- before the first card is drawn — which is exactly why the refusal is
  -- declared rather than assumed away: a rule that is never reached is still
  -- the answer a player reaching for that card is owed.
  REFUSE cardType(needs: "a card that is not a Leader Card") UNLESS count(FROM $card "non-leader card") >= 1
  label: "Charge"
  text: "the turn player may place 1 card from their hand in their Energy Area, face up (7-2-11)"

-- The refusal this one does *not* carry is the legacy engine's "you have
-- already had your charge this turn" (`oncePerTurn`), which it gives for every
-- card in hand once the Main Phase has begun. Saying that here means declaring
-- the charge in the Main Phase too and refusing it there, and the condition
-- that tells the two apart is "which question is on the table" — which the
-- language has no word for and which is not worth a `Cond` kind of its own
-- before #142 brings the evaluator both engines' card rules share. Until then
-- the once-per-turn fact is the *step*: `chargeEnergy` (7-2-11) is asked once a
-- turn, and a phase this move is not declared in never offers it.

DEFINE ACTION endMain                                                     -- §7-3-5
  WHEN [main]
  prompts: [main]
  DO {}
  label: "End turn"
  text: "the turn player ends their Main Phase, and with it the last phase they act in (7-3-5)"

DEFINE ACTION pass                                                        -- §7-3-4
  WHEN [setup, charge, main]
  prompts: [mulligan, charge, main]
  DO {}
  listed: false
  label: "Pass"
  text: "take none of what the question offers, whatever it is (6-2-1-9-1, 7-2-11, 7-3-4)"

-- The one move that is not an answer to the question on the table: it is taken
-- at any point in a game, including at a prompt that belongs to the other
-- player, and no card may cause or replace it (0-1-3-4). So `WHEN` is every
-- phase a game is still being played in, and `prompts:` is left out.
--
-- Its `DO` is empty because a game *ending* is not something the effect
-- language can say — no op of `OP_SCHEMA` ends one — so the ending stays the
-- interpreter's, the way the steps in `vm/flow.ts`'s `STEP_WORK` are, and this
-- declaration is what decides the move may be taken at all. A `DO` written here
-- would be a program `vm/index.ts` refuses by name rather than ignores.

DEFINE ACTION concede                                                     -- §0-1-3-4
  WHEN [setup, charge, main, mainEnd, end]
  DO {}
  listed: false
  label: "Concede"
  text: "a player gives up the game, and their opponent wins immediately (0-1-3-4)"

-- ── playing a card (§5-5, §8-3-2, §13-2, §16-2) ────────────────────────────
--
-- Three paragraphs rather than one, because the shared `Action` union has
-- three words: a client sends `play`, `playUnison` or `playZ`, and the
-- declaration's **name is the action type** (`vm/actions.ts`). What they share
-- is the `DO`: every one of them is the `play` op, which is 5-5-3's "play this
-- card" — so a play a player declares and a play a skill makes are the same
-- act, resolved in one place (`vm/play.ts`), and neither of them pends a
-- trigger by name. The card arriving is `moved(asPlay: true)` and
-- `triggers.rules` decides that this is `played`, `youPlayed` and
-- `opponentPlayed`; its [Permanent]s stand because it is in an in-play area.
--
-- **What the `REFUSE` lists do not say yet, and why.** The legacy engine's
-- `whyNotPlay` has two gates beyond the price, and the language can say
-- neither: 22-39's [Unique] needs a filter for "a card with the same name as
-- *this* candidate", which `FILTER_FIELDS` has no field for (the keyword's own
-- body is #154's), and 20-14's prohibitions need an effect in force to read,
-- which `vm/effects.ts` still hands to #145 (`DEFERRED_STATICS.forbid`). So
-- the price is the first refusal and today the only one — which is the same
-- first refusal the legacy engine gives on every board that has neither of
-- those in force, and is a board the rules engine is *wider* than the manual
-- on until they land.
--
-- The other half the price cannot settle is an **X cost**: 1-2-2-2 makes its
-- value an answer to a question, and a candidate is a card and nothing else,
-- so `costOf` is absent and `vm/costs.ts` refuses such a card as `unread`
-- rather than offering it free. [Evolve], [Union], [Over Realm] and [Swap] are
-- plays a keyword gates and are #157's.

DEFINE ACTION play                                                        -- §8-3-2
  WHEN [main]
  prompts: [main]
  FOR 1 IN you.hand "battle card"
  BIND "card"
  COST [energy]
  DO {
    play(target: $card)
  }
  again: true
  label: "Play"
  text: "the turn player pays a Battle Card's energy cost and places it in their Battle Area, active and revealed (8-3-2, 5-5)"

-- 13-2-3: a Unison Card arrives carrying the energy that was paid for it as
-- markers, which is what `X` is in this program — the amount the move's price
-- was settled at (`runProgram`). One paragraph covers only the Unisons whose
-- printed cost is a number: a Unison printed `X` lets its master choose how
-- many markers to buy (13-2-2), and that choice is the `x` a candidate has
-- nowhere to carry.
DEFINE ACTION playUnison                                                  -- §13-2
  WHEN [main]
  prompts: [main]
  FOR 1 IN you.hand "unison card"
  BIND "card"
  COST [energy]
  DO {
    play(target: $card)
    addMarker(target: $card, n: X)
  }
  again: true
  label: "Play Unison"
  text: "the turn player pays a Unison Card's cost and places it in their Unison Area with that many markers, sending the Unison already there to the Drop Area (13-2, 3-11-5)"

-- 16-2 and 17-2: a Z-card is played from the Z-Deck and pays a Z-Energy price
-- as well as its energy cost, which is why this is the one move that names two
-- prices — in that order, because that is the order the engine that has been
-- playing charges them in and the order is what the oracle compares.
--
-- 6-1-4: a Z-Leader is not played at all, it arrives through [Z-Awaken]; and
-- 18-2's Z-Unison is a Unison, so its markers are the `x` the paragraph above
-- says a candidate cannot carry. Both are refused here as the cards this move
-- is not about, which is the honest answer while it is about the other two.
DEFINE ACTION playZ                                                       -- §16-2
  WHEN [main]
  prompts: [main]
  FOR 1 IN you.zDeck "Z-card"
  BIND "card"
  COST [zEnergy, energy]
  DO {
    play(target: $card)
  }
  REFUSE cardType(needs: "a Z-Battle Card or a Z-Extra Card") UNLESS any([count(FROM $card "Z-battle card") >= 1, count(FROM $card "Z-extra card") >= 1])
  again: true
  label: "Play"
  text: "the turn player pays a Z-card's Z-Energy price and its energy cost and places it in their Battle Area; a Z-Extra replaces the Z-Extras already there (16-2, 17-2-1-3)"
